    {
        "content": "<p>Try <code>deriv_fun_pow</code> instead of <code>deriv_pow</code>; or use <code>erw</code> instead of <code>rw</code>.</p>",
        "id": 581189796,
        "sender_full_name": "David Loeffler",
        "timestamp": 1774289857
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Explanation: this is a slightly unsatisfactory issue about the calculus library, which is that many lemmas about functions can be written either in \"purely functional\" form (like <code>f ∘ g</code>) or in \"eta-expanded\" form (like <code>fun x ↦ f (g x)</code>). Most lemmas are available in both versions, but it takes a bit of practice to know which one you should be using. </p>\n<p>Why is this needed? The basic answer is that \"detecting when things are equal or not\" is hard. Different Lean tactics use at different \"transparency levels\", meaning roughly that they have more or less power to detect when things are equal. For efficiency reasons, rewriting tactics like <code>rw</code> and <code>simp</code> operate at a fairly low transparency level (because they have to make <em>lots</em> of attempted matches so they can only afford to spend a small amount of effort on each). Regrettably it means they can't tell that <code>f ∘ g</code> and <code>fun x ↦ f (g x)</code> are the same function, so they can't apply lemmas about one when the goal contains the other. The <code>erw</code> tactic is a variant of <code>rw</code> which uses a different transparency setting, and this <em>does</em> work in your example.</p>",
        "id": 581193451,
        "sender_full_name": "David Loeffler",
        "timestamp": 1774290840
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>Visually, the pattern matches, but the tactic state shows a mismatch between <code>fun t</code> and <code>fun x</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That isn't (only) what the diff shows; if I format it as a diff you can hopefully see more clearly:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Diff\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"gd\">-deriv ((fun t ↦ log (V t / G)) ^ 2) t</span>\n<span class=\"gi\">+deriv (fun x ↦ log (V x / G) ^ 2) t</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The parenthesization is also different (which David elaborates on the implication of above). The name mismatch is irrelevant.</p>",
        "id": 581202645,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1774293662
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I want to sincerely thank all of you for your help.  <br>\nYour guidance allowed me to finally complete the proof and build my project successfully.  <br>\nThe last steps were subtle, but thanks to your attention and explanations, I was able to understand the normalisation issues and finish the theorem properly.</p>\n<p>Here is the final build trace confirming that everything now compiles on my side:</p>\n<p>evangelos@EvangelosWifi lean4-mvp-1 % lake build urfWizard<br>\n    ⚠ [2096/2097] Built urfWizard<br>\n   warning: urfWizard.lean:209:8: try 'simp' instead of 'simpa'<br>\n  Note: This linter can be disabled with <code>set_option linter.unnecessarySimpa false</code><br>\n<strong>Build completed successfully (2097 jobs).</strong></p>\n<p>Thank you again — your support made a real difference.</p>",
